  }))), "flex team"), mdx("p", null, "Until July, I led and executed a project in the Web Client Platform Team (WCP) to end the regular release cycle, which had been the biggest bottleneck in our web product delivery process. Judging only by the outcome, it was a good thing. But it required making extremely sound decisions on matters that would determine the future of the codebase. The process was long and grueling, and many moments of execution were risky. I lived in constant tension, gradually getting mentally exhausted. It was an extension of the state I wrote about in last year\u2019s retrospective. \u201CIf I didn\u2019t pull myself together and make good decisions in a good state, I couldn\u2019t do anything properly.\u201D"), mdx("p", null, "After it wrapped up, I gave a talk at a developer conference in August. Monorepos and code governance are topics that make for good talks, so the audience response on site was strong. It was the largest crowd I\u2019ve ever spoken in front of in my life. I was delighted that we embedded a QR code for our team\u2019s hiring post into the YouTube video. Around then, my mental energy was depleted, and every day felt slightly like I might throw up. I think it was because there wasn\u2019t really a clear moment to catch my breath even after finishing something big."), mdx("p", null, "In the fall, I took a single LLM-calling API from the server side and, in something like a playground, kept building demos with a fellow PM and engineers\u2014whatever they suggested like \u201CCould we do this or that with AI inside the product?\u201D\u2014to get people\u2019s heads turning. In the flex product, you could say, \u201CMake an Acid Rain game in the name of a member of our company,\u201D and the AI would make it\u2014stuff like that. I wrote a lot of prompts and started thinking seriously, around that time, about how to integrate AI into a product well. It was the most unusual kind of work I\u2019d done within the team. It reminded me that making software is, roughly speaking, a way to turn imagination into reality\u2014and that I like that. I\u2019m most wary, in life, of cynicism; good imagination lives the farthest away from cynicism."), mdx("p", null, "In the last quarter, I joined a squad building an AI Agent product. After 3 years and 9 months in the developer platform organization, I transferred into a product-building squad. Starting in October, I switched into a mode of working long hours to ship. While working 50+ hours a week, I\u2019m exploring\u2014and trying various things\u2014around how to live happily without being filled with anger, and how to savor the beauty of the world. I\u2019ll probably live in this mode through the entire first half of next year. When I first joined the platform team, the entire platform org was three people; now it\u2019s over twenty. I\u2019ve also contributed a lot\u2014head-first\u2014to the team\u2019s goal-setting, systems, ways of working, and DNA. I hope many colleagues feel fulfillment and grow well on top of that foundation."), mdx("h1", {
