  }))), "Development"), mdx("p", null, "Like many people, I can\u2019t talk about development this year without talking about AI. The second half of the year was when I fully introduced AI assistants, led by Claude Code, into my workflow. The trigger was something I heard when meeting a developer friend: \u201CAn intern at our company doesn\u2019t know development at all, but they\u2019re vibe-coding all the way to git commits\u2014and the product is getting built. It\u2019s insane. If developers can\u2019t ride this wave, they\u2019ll become Heungseon Daewongun.\u201D I\u2019d tried it only in a half-hearted way and gotten disappointed a lot, but that was when I decided I needed to go all in."), mdx("p", null, "I tried to shift my workflow to delegate as much as possible to AI. I consciously tried to take my hands off the keyboard, even at the cost of short-term productivity loss. I wrote instruction prompts in the ticket body in Linear and let it vibe. If I didn\u2019t like it, I\u2019d delete everything, revise the ticket body, and make it start over from the beginning. I repeated this until I got an output I was satisfied with. After living like that for about a month, a plausible CLAUDE.md emerged from the process, and Claude Code latched tightly onto my flow. Now I use about 200 million tokens per week (per ccusage). It\u2019s great and fortunate that I work at a company where I can use Claude Code practically without limits."), mdx("p", null, "In the first half, I was very interested in the latest approaches to Micro Frontends and in strengthening polyrepo governance, and I tried to keep up. In the second half, AI became my primary exploration path. I was interested in using AI for development, but I was also interested in attaching AI to an existing product\u2014something I felt was comparatively less explored in the ecosystem. I also studied how to build Generative UI well and how to design chat UIs. Toward the end of the year, I returned to a product squad, and it\u2019s been a long time since I last built product. I opened Figma for the first time in a long while, and it\u2019s been a while since I last talked with designers. I\u2019m trying to combine the latest approaches to global state management, styling, and data management with the approaches I prefer, and with what AI products require. I feel a bit disoriented after being assigned to the squad, but I want to do well even in this newly given environment and opportunity."), mdx("p", null, "I\u2019m slowly working on a personal project. Right now it\u2019s an AI application that takes notes on articles and books I\u2019ve read, uses them as material to write prose, and sends the result via email. I\u2019m building it to automate blog writing and to publish AI-written posts on the blog as well. To delegate the work to AI, I had to make my writing process concrete and explainable. I ended up thinking a lot about how I write. If I just threw in references and said, \u201CWrite an article about this,\u201D the quality was never high. I had to explain everything: from choosing the topic, to selecting references that fit the topic, to choosing a writing method aligned with both the topic and references, to choosing and explaining an editing method aligned with that writing method. Only then could I prompt in a way similar to how I write. Now I\u2019ve split those processes out and am improving prompt quality. Writing is far harder than coding because it\u2019s difficult to set a verifiable reward, and it\u2019s very labor-intensive. This year, I want to produce something I\u2019m satisfied with and show it to people."), mdx("h1", {
