  }))), "People"), mdx("p", null, "Even in 2025, if someone asked for a coffee chat, I usually showed up. I basically fixed the location at the Mahogany Cafe on the first floor of Homeplus at Jamsil Station and kept meeting people. In the first half, I met people I see every year, and it was nice to see people who had moved from our team to other companies."), mdx("p", null, "After getting hyped from the conference, I rode the momentum and did about 13 private coffee chats. Then I met too many people and reached a state where I could no longer talk to anyone. I\u2019m a very introverted person (INFJ), but I also like talking and going out, so I receive all the mental shock and fatigue in full\u2014an odd personality. From 4Q, I couldn\u2019t even reply properly; I\u2019m very sorry. It\u2019s because I pushed myself too hard. I have no promised timeline, but I will reply to everyone\u2026"), mdx("p", null, "A lot of people struggled at work, and hiring isn\u2019t going well. In situations like that\u2014when someone else is having a hard time\u2014I often wondered how I could possibly dare to give advice. To people struggling at work, I said: challenge yourself enough, but protect yourself. For job hunting, I had nothing to say. In most cases, even by my own judgment, there wasn\u2019t anything more they \u201Cshould\u201D be doing\u2014they were already preparing extremely well. And yet, if they still want to build a career as a developer, there\u2019s nothing else to do but keep going and keep knocking. It\u2019s really hard."), mdx("p", null, "I want to meet more people from roles other than developers. In the summer, I attended a homecoming event for seniors from my university\u2019s independent press, where I used to be an editor-in-chief. I received dozens of business cards from people who had already become reporters. For them, meeting developers was so rare that they found me fascinating and didn\u2019t know what to ask. In that sense, the way conversations worked changed completely, and I felt a big sense of freshness from having conversations I\u2019d never had before. I also felt respect\u2014so many people are walking a path I gave up."), mdx("p", null, "An absurd number of people got married. When I counted, I went to 13 weddings this year. One of my closest friends\u2014someone I\u2019ve known for over 10 years\u2014also got married, and I gave my highest wedding gift amount ever. I still enjoy seeing one of the happiest scenes in people\u2019s lives. But after paying wedding gifts so often, I started wondering if I\u2019ve become part of this wedding ecosystem\u2014since some unknown point in time\u2014without realizing it. Will this strange sense of belonging end up making me get married? I don\u2019t know. I\u2019ll think about it next year."), mdx("h1", {
